.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt NANOSLEEP 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nanosleep
.Nd suspend the calling process for an interval
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In time.h
.Ft int
.Fn nanosleep "const struct timespec *req" "struct timespec *rem"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn nanosleep
suspends the calling process for the interval in
.Fa req .
The
.Va tv_sec
field specifies seconds and
.Va tv_nsec
specifies nanoseconds.
Seconds must be nonnegative; nanoseconds must be from 0 through 999999999.
.Pp
The kernel rounds the interval up to whole 10 ms ticks and blocks the
process until its tick deadline.
Other runnable processes continue to execute.
If a signal interrupts the sleep, the call fails with
.Er EINTR
and stores the time left to the rounded deadline in
.Fa rem ,
unless that pointer is null.
The remainder has 10 ms resolution.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call number is 162.
EBX points to the request and ECX to the optional remainder.
The kernel timespec consists of two signed 32-bit fields: seconds,
then nanoseconds.
The kernel limits the seconds used in the tick calculation to 21474836
and the resulting interval to 2147483647 ticks.
.Pp
The raw call writes a zero remainder on normal completion.
If interrupted, it attempts to write the remainder but returns
.Er EINTR
even if that copy fails.
.Pp
musl converts its public timespec to the kernel's 32-bit structure.
It copies the remainder back to the caller only on
.Er EINTR ;
on success, the caller's
.Fa rem
is unchanged.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn nanosleep
returns 0 on completion, or -1 on failure and sets
.Va errno .
The raw kernel call returns a negative error number on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/timeprobe.c
requests a 150 ms sleep through system call 162 after forking a child.
It checks the elapsed interval and that the child made progress while
the parent slept.
.Sh ERRORS
The kernel handler returns:
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The request is null or cannot be read, there is no current process,
or the remainder cannot be written after normal completion.
The musl wrapper reads the public request and writes the public
remainder in user space.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A pending signal interrupted the sleep.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
Seconds are negative, or nanoseconds are outside the permitted range.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr gettimeofday 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2
